<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Upper Lusatia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> is a historical region on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Neisse</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Görlitz</a>, today divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Settled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a>, it was long held by the Bohemian crown, while its six leading towns formed the Lusatian League in 1346. It passed to the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> in 1635 and remains home to a Sorbian minority.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia.jpg" alt="Upper Lusatia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J.-H. Janßen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bautzen_Hammermuehle_44.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-lusatia">Lower Lusatia</a> to the north, makes up the region of Lusatia, named after the Slavic Lusici tribe. Most of it lies in the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, with an eastern part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Neisse</a>. Its historic capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a>, and its largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Görlitz</a>, halved between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and Poland since 1945. The name Lusatia superior was first recorded in a deed of 1474; earlier the territory was called Milsko, after the West Slavic Milceni tribe. The south is shaped by the Lusatian Mountains, the westernmost range of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetes">Sudetes</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a> rises in the Lusatian Highlands and flows through Bautzen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> settled the region from the 7th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milceni">Milceni</a> had a fortified centre where the Ortenburg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> now stands. They were forced to pay tribute in 932 and subdued by Otto I in 939. Bautzen was first mentioned in 1002, and the Peace of Bautzen of 30 January 1018 assigned the Milceni lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, though German rule returned by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> in 1033. In the 12th century Upper Lusatia came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> under Přemyslid rule, and in the first century of that rule all its major towns and religious institutions apart from Bautzen were founded. German peasants settled alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostsiedlung">Ostsiedlung</a>.</p><p>The Ascanian margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> held it between 1253 and 1262, and in 1268 Margrave Otto IV divided it into the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Görlitz</a>. King John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> incorporated Görlitz into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Bohemian Crown</a> in 1329. In 1346 five royal cities and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zittau">Zittau</a> founded the Lusatian League, supported by King Charles IV, and the six towns gained political influence against the nobility.</p><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-hungary">Louis II of Bohemia</a> at Mohács in 1526, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-austria">Ferdinand I of Austria</a> inherited the region. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-prague-1635">Peace of Prague</a> of 1635 transferred it to the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, and in 1815 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave the north-eastern part to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was fixed at the Oder-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Neisse</a> line, which split the historic region between the two countries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a>, a West Slavic minority, still live in Upper Lusatia, and about 20,000 people between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamenz">Kamenz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> and Hoyerswerda speak Sorbian. Muskau Park, at Bad Muskau and Łęknica, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, and the Sorbian Museum in Bautzen and the Lusatian Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Zgorzelec</a> are dedicated to the region's history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Upper Lusatia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Textiles, linen from <b>Upper Lusatia</b>, glass and, after 1710, porcelain from the Meissen manufactory developed into important industries, making Saxony a forerunner of German industrialisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Upper Lusatia</b> passed to the Electorate of Saxony in 1635, and the league lingered until 1815, when Görlitz and Lauban went to Prussia.</blockquote><a href="/state/lusatian-league">Lusatian League</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 15th century the region came to be called <b>Upper Lusatia</b>, in contrast to Lower Lusatia.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-upper-lusatia">Margraviate of Upper Lusatia</a></li><li><blockquote>A Catholic remnant in <b>Upper Lusatia</b> continued at Bautzen and became the modern Diocese of Dresden-Meissen.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-meissen">Prince-Bishopric of Meissen</a></li><li><blockquote>Their territory included the regions later called Daleminzia, <b>Milzenland</b> (Upper Lusatia) and Lusatia.</blockquote><a href="/state/sorbs">Sorbs</a></li></ul></section>
